---
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import SuitGlyph from '../components/SuitGlyph.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

import { CUE_COUNT, cuesInSuit } from '../data/cues';
import { SUIT_IDS, SUITS } from '../data/suits';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Sidesteps is, how to use it, and where the idea comes from."
>
  <article class="container-page pt-10 sm:pt-16">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="About"
      title="Small, sideways nudges for hard problems."
    >
      Sidesteps is a deck of {CUE_COUNT} short cues. None of them will solve
      your problem for you. They’re meant to knock your thinking a few degrees
      off its usual track.
    </PageHeader>

    <div class="mt-16 grid gap-16 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_20rem] lg:gap-24">
      <div class="prose-page max-w-2xl">
        <h2>Why it works</h2>
        <p>
          When you’re stuck, it’s usually not for lack of effort. You’ve been
          circling the same few approaches, and each lap digs the groove a
          little deeper. A random prompt from outside that groove forces a
          different question: <em>what would this look like if…?</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          The cues are deliberately short and open. “Salt” or “Look at the
          fruit” can mean something different for a stubborn bug, a stalled
          essay, or a difficult conversation. The interpretation is where the
          useful thinking happens.
        </p>

        <h2>How to use it</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Name the problem first.</strong> Even one sentence sharpens
            what you’re looking for.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Take the first reading that moves you.</strong> There’s no
            correct interpretation. If a card points the wrong way, notice why;
            that’s often informative too.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Don’t over-draw.</strong> One card held for a few minutes
            beats ten skimmed in a row.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep what lands.</strong> Save useful cards to your notebook
            and write down what they made you think of.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Some cards contradict each other. <em>Maximize</em> and{' '}
          <em>Try minimalism</em>, <em>Overwhelming force</em> and{' '}
          <em>Restraint</em>. That’s on purpose: deciding which one applies is
          itself a way of seeing the problem more clearly.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the idea comes from</h2>
        <p>
          Sidesteps owes an obvious debt to <em>Oblique Strategies</em>, the
          card deck Brian Eno and Peter Schmidt made in 1975 to help artists
          through creative blocks. This deck takes the same idea and points it
          at everyday problems: practical, technical, and personal, as well as
          creative.
        </p>

        <h2>Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          There are no accounts. Whatever you type, and the cards you keep, are
          stored only in your browser’s local storage.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose mt-12">
          <a href="/?draw" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
            <Icon name="redo" />
            Draw a card
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="self-start lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:pt-2">
        <h2 class="eyebrow">The seven suits</h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
          {SUIT_IDS.map((id) => (
            <li data-suit={id}>
              <a
                href={`/deck/#${id}`}
                class="group flex items-start gap-3 py-4"
              >
                <SuitGlyph suit={id} class="mt-1 size-3.5 text-suit" />
                <span class="flex-1">
                  <span class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                    <span class="font-semibold group-hover:text-accent">
                      {SUITS[id].name}
                    </span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-ink-faint tabular-nums">
                      {cuesInSuit(id).length} cards
                    </span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="mt-0.5 block text-sm text-ink-soft">
                    {SUITS[id].description}
                  </span>
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose-page {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .prose-page :global(h2) {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 480;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .prose-page > :global(h2:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .prose-page :global(p + p) {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .prose-page :global(ul) {
    margin-block: 1.25rem;
    padding-left: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .prose-page :global(li) {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-page :global(li + li) {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .prose-page :global(li)::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0.25rem;
    top: 0.72em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .prose-page :global(strong) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
